WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.620
- Even to this day,

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:04.320
we continue to follow the music.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.540
We work with the most
famous drummers in the world

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.340
to make the sounds that they need,

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.515
so they can make music.

00:00:09.515 --> 00:00:12.348
(cymbals tapping)

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:16.320
(drums beating)

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.610
My name's Paul Francis,
and I'm a cymbal maker

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.333
for the Avedis Zildjian company.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.610
Cymbal making has remained the same

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.460
for almost 400 years.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.680
Zildjian Cymbal goes back to 1623.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.860
We're part of this
legacy of cymbal making,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.191
and it's really, really exciting

00:00:35.191 --> 00:00:37.657
that we're continuing this history.

00:00:37.657 --> 00:00:39.610
(cymbals crashing)

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.383
We make well over 600 models
in different diameters,

00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:46.180
and it's much like if you're a carpenter,

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.230
you don't have one tool, you
have a toolbox full of tools.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.570
So, as a drummer, the cymbals
are part of your toolbox.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.580
OK, you have a big cymbal like this,

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.880
it's called a ride cymbal,
so you keep time on it.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.270
These are hi-hats, and
these are two cymbals

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.250
that come together.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:02.490
Then you have crash cymbals,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.994
and these all offer
different sound colors,

00:01:04.994 --> 00:01:08.630
so the drummer can express
himself in a nonverbal way

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.305
within the context of the piece of music.

00:01:11.305 --> 00:01:14.690
(drum cymbal music)

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.845
Cymbal making is a lot
like being a blacksmith

00:01:16.845 --> 00:01:18.270
or a farrier.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.130
There's a lot of steps that
go into making a cymbal

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.838
and, you know, it could
take a couple days.

00:01:23.838 --> 00:01:25.160
(cymbal crashing)

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:26.270
We're gonna take that casting;

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.083
we're gonna throw it in an oven.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.640
And we're gonna shape it;

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:32.766
we're gonna hammer it;

00:01:32.766 --> 00:01:35.179
we're gonna shave it;
we're gonna lathe it;

00:01:35.179 --> 00:01:37.235
goes down to a cymbal
test, so he plays it.

00:01:37.235 --> 00:01:38.650
(cymbal crashes)

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.055
- Sound quality, cosmetic quality,

00:01:41.055 --> 00:01:42.455
we look for it all.

00:01:42.455 --> 00:01:44.510
(cymbal crashes)

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.750
- I've had the privilege to work with

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.330
a lot of great drummers.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:50.273
Steve Gadd, Elvin Jones,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.840
Zak Starkey, who was
actually Ringo Starr's son.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.810
Zak plays in The Who and
a variety of other bands.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:58.740
And Carter Beauford,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.410
Carter Beauford plays for
the Dave Matthews Band.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.297
The list goes on and on.

00:02:03.297 --> 00:02:06.340
(dramatic cymbal crashing)

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.300
I've been here for 30 years.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.419
It's really fantastic
that I'm actually part

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:14.280
of the world of music and music
that's going to be created

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.040
or has been created, and
it's my job right now

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.660
to make sure that my
knowledge gets passed on

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.360
to the next people that
are gonna continue it

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.893
for the next 400 years.

00:02:24.893 --> 00:02:28.893
(various drum and cymbal music)

